
  Before that, one fork decides the shape of all four.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether this app has a server. It isn't a preference — it determines whether notifications mean anything at
  all.

  - [1mClient-only[0m (static page, localStorage): cheapest, deploys anywhere, no accounts to build. But there is exactly one
    user and they are the only one who can change anything — so every notification tells you about a thing you just did
    yourself. Notifications are close to decorative. Rules out cross-user anything without a rewrite.
  - [1mServer-backed[0m (API + database + real accounts): notifications become genuinely useful — someone else changes a task,
    you hear about it. Costs an auth system, a datastore, a deploy target, and roughly 3–4× the work before the first
    notification ever fires. Hard to retrofit onto a client-only app later; easy to keep small at the start.
  - [1mLocal-first with sync later[0m: build tasks client-only now with a data model shaped for sync, add the server when it's
    needed. Defers the cost, keeps the door open, but the deferred work is real and half-done sync is a classic tarpit.

  [1mMy recommendation: server-backed[0m — but only if multi-user is actually the point. If this is a personal task list, I'd
  say skip notifications entirely rather than build a server to support them, and that's a legitimate answer to give
  you.
